 "why_it_matters": [
  {
   "text": "Steeper ramps take more effort. Wheelchair users pushing up a {{slope=0.05241|3°}} slope used more force, torque and cadence than on the level (36 users with spinal cord injury).",
   "evidence": "in-library",
   "refs": [
    "REF-00388"
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "The effort lands on the arms and shoulders. A 34-study review found slope effects under-studied.",
   "evidence": "in-library",
   "refs": [
    "REF-00037"
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "People with multiple sclerosis spend more energy walking, and uphill feels harder than the measured cost suggests.",
   "evidence": "in-library",
   "refs": [
    "REF-00753",
    "REF-00745"
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Going down matters too. At {{slope=1/12|1:12}} and {{slope=1/8|1:8}} most users coped, but power-chair users and people with vision impairment raised safety concerns on steeper descents.",
   "evidence": "in-library",
   "refs": [
    "REF-00742"
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Climbing a short ramp is not the same as managing it all day. In a 171-person trial only a few manual wheelchair users could not climb {{slope=1/8|1:8}} over {{len=9144|30 ft}}; it tested one ramp, not sustained use.",
   "evidence": "in-library",
   "refs": [
    "REF-00752"
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Codes set maximum slopes as gentle as {{slope=1/16.7|1:16.7}}, and several prefer {{slope=1/20|1:20}} or gentler. A legal limit is the least a code allows, not the best a design can do.",
   "evidence": "regulatory",
   "refs": []
  }
 ],
